      "math_academy_id": "e-464",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
